
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://as.nyu.edu/faculty/thomas-nagel.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Thomas Nagel</a> is the 2026 winner of the $1 million Berggruen Prize for Philosophy &amp; Culture.</p>



<span id="more-27571"></span>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full is-resized"><img decoding="async" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/nagel.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-26694" style="aspect-ratio:0.8534304717610135;width:396px;height:auto"/></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Berggruen Institute called Nagel, professor emeritus of philosophy and law at New York University, &#8220;one of the most significant philosophers of our time,&#8221; and praising his &#8220;long-lasting contributions through his scholarly work and commentary in the public sphere,&#8221; referencing his work in moral philosophy, legal and political theory, metaphysics, and philosophy of mind, and his contributions to our understanding of consciousness, existence, altruism, equality, justice, and free will.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a <a href="https://berggruen.org/news/usd1-million-berggruen-philosophy-prize-awarded-to-thomas-nagel" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">press release</a>, Nicolas Berggruen, the chairman and founder of the Berggruen Institute, says, &#8220;The Institute is pleased to recognize Thomas Nagel for his interrogation of the human experience and for expanding consciousness and our capacity to understand ourselves.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Berggruen Prize, according to the Institute, &#8220;seeks to place philosophical thought of the highest caliber on center stage of the public discourse and to amplify its significance to society.&#8221; It was created in 2016 and is awarded annually. </p>



